The current proposal of $186,250,000 for New York is woefully inadequate given the gravity of our situation. Our satellite imagery reveals a dense urban fabric where nearly every block represents vulnerable populations and high-value infrastructure assets at risk from climate hazards — heat, flooding, storm surge, etc. The FEMA NRI confirms this: we carry a composite hazard risk score of 97.3/100 — the highest possible tier — and a Social Vulnerability Index (SoVI) of 0.90, meaning nearly all residents are highly vulnerable to climate impacts. We cannot afford to split the $300M budget equally or even proportionally with San Francisco when our exposure is orders of magnitude higher.

We demand a reallocation that reflects our actual risk profile — not an arbitrary split. Based on hazard severity and population vulnerability, New York should receive at least 85% of the total available funds: $255,000,000. This would allow us to deploy critical infrastructure upgrades, emergency response capacity, and community adaptation measures before disasters strike again.

